## Solution

We evaluate $\displaystyle I = \oint_{|z|=2} \frac{e^{1/z}}{z(z^2+1)}\,dz$ by the residue theorem. The integrand has singularities at $z=0$, $z=i$, $z=-i$, all lying inside $|z|=2$. Thus

$$I = 2\pi i\Big(\operatorname{Res}_{z=0} f + \operatorname{Res}_{z=i} f + \operatorname{Res}_{z=-i} f\Big).$$

**Residues at $z=\pm i$ (simple poles).**

$$\operatorname{Res}_{z=i} f = \frac{e^{1/i}}{i\cdot(i+i)} = \frac{e^{-i}}{2i^2} = -\frac{e^{-i}}{2}, \qquad \operatorname{Res}_{z=-i} f = \frac{e^{1/(-i)}}{(-i)(-i-i)} = \frac{e^{i}}{2i^2} = -\frac{e^{i}}{2}.$$

Their sum is $-\dfrac{e^{i}+e^{-i}}{2} = -\cos 1$.

**Residue at $z=0$ (essential singularity).** For $|z|<1$,

$$\frac{1}{1+z^2} = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-1)^n z^{2n}, \qquad e^{1/z} = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{z^{-k}}{k!}.$$

Hence

$$f(z) = \frac{e^{1/z}}{z}\cdot\frac{1}{1+z^2} = \left(\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{z^{-k}}{k!}\right)\left(\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-1)^n z^{2n-1}\right).$$

The coefficient of $z^{-1}$ requires $-k+2n-1=-1$, i.e. $k=2n$. Therefore

$$\operatorname{Res}_{z=0} f = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^n}{(2n)!} = \cos 1.$$

**Total.**

$$\operatorname{Res}_{z=0} f + \operatorname{Res}_{z=i} f + \operatorname{Res}_{z=-i} f = \cos 1 - \cos 1 = 0.$$

Consequently

$$\oint_{|z|=2} \frac{e^{1/z}}{z(z^2+1)}\,dz = 2\pi i \cdot 0 = 0.$$

The integral **is equal to zero**. The residue at the essential singularity $z=0$ exactly cancels the combined residues at the two simple poles $z=\pm i$.

